<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#military-industrial-complex">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Military-industrial complex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The military-industrial complex is the network of armed forces, arms manufacturers and political interests that grows up around a permanent defence establishment. The term was popularised by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a>, who warned in his farewell address of January 1961 against its 'unwarranted influence' on American government. It has since become a standard term in debates over defence spending and its influence on policy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The expression military–industrial complex describes the relationship between a country's military and the defence industry that supplies it, seen together as a vested interest that influences public policy. Both sides benefit, one by obtaining weapons and the other by being paid to supply them. The concept is generally treated as unique to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and its use for other countries is ambiguous. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a> used the term in his Farewell Address to the Nation on 17 January 1961, saying that the conjunction of an immense military establishment and a large arms industry was new in American experience, and that government must guard against the acquisition of unwarranted influence by the complex.</p><p>The speech was written by Ralph E. Williams and Malcolm Moos, and how far <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> and his brother Milton shaped it is unclear from the surviving documents. Eisenhower was neither the first to use the phrase nor the first to warn of the danger. The first known use was by Winfield W. Riefler in 1947, who used it of the economic potentials of the states at war. C. Wright Mills's 1956 book The Power Elite, which described a powerful elite of military and corporate chiefs set against a powerless mass society, served as a framework for the debate of the 1960s and 1970s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In its later meanings the phrase moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a>'s warning of a future danger to a claim that the complex already shaped policy. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>, opponents used it, partly because Eisenhower's conservative reputation protected them from charges of disloyalty, as John Kenneth Galbraith said. The Port Huron Statement used it closer to Mills's sense of a power elite. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> spoke in a 1963 sermon of millions of citizens disturbed that the military-industrial complex too often shaped national policy. Dow Chemical became linked with the war after 1966 through its contract to make napalm, and protesters picketed its offices and disrupted shareholder meetings, among them Honeywell's in 1970.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Congress passed the Mansfield Amendment of 1969 to restrict military-sponsored university research, and Stanford and MIT spun off such work into SRI International and Draper Laboratory. Senator William Proxmire became the chief advocate of the view of the complex as an unaccountable bureaucracy, and in 1968 he named 23 defence contractors in a press conference. The economist Seymour Melman argued for converting arms industries to civilian work, while Murray Weidenbaum held that dependence on government contracts harms the contractors themselves. The phrase is often used as a pejorative, and some scholars say it suggests a conspiracy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Military–industrial complex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He commanded the Allied invasion of Europe in 1944 and served two terms as the 34th US president, warning on leaving office of the '<b>military-industrial complex</b>'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-14">14 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
